---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT, DOMAIN, REPO } from '../site';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · Trailscope"
  description={`What ${DOMAIN} collects (only your waitlist email, if you give it) and what Trailscope itself sends anywhere (nothing).`}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Last updated 9 November 2026</p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      {DOMAIN} has no analytics, no tracking and no cookies, and loads nothing from other sites. The
      server keeps standard access logs (IP address, page, time and browser) for up to 14 days to keep
      the site running and to stop abuse.
    </p>

    <h2>The Trailscope Cloud waitlist</h2>
    <p>
      If you join the waitlist, we store your email address, the plan you picked, the traffic range
      you chose and the time you signed up. We use them only to tell you when Trailscope Cloud opens
      and what it will cost, and to plan capacity. We don't share them with anyone and don't send a
      newsletter.
    </p>
    <p>
      The data is stored on our own server in Frankfurt, Germany. We delete it when the Cloud has
      launched and we've written to you, or earlier if you ask. To see or delete what we hold about
      you, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Self-hosted Trailscope</h2>
    <p>
      The open-source Trailscope you run yourself sends nothing to us: no telemetry, no usage
      statistics, no license checks. The requests it records stay in your own databases. Its
      <a href="/docs/security/">security page</a> explains what it collects from your applications
      and how to limit it.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> or open an issue on
      <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
